  ],
  "text": "London sits further north than Newfoundland, and runs about ten degrees warmer. Sunlight does not explain that. Both places get the same amount. The difference is a river running inside the ocean, and it is enormous. Warm water runs north along the surface, giving up heat the whole way. Cold water that has lost its warmth is saltier, and salty water is heavy. So it sinks, and keeps going, all the way back south along the seafloor. One full lap of that circuit takes about a thousand years. But melting ice adds fresh water, and fresh water is not heavy enough to sink. Which is the actual machine behind every headline about Europe getting colder. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
